{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"16d4416930d96460","type":"text","text":"## ## Idea\n\nA Cold Turkey Blocker-inspired focus app for Linux.\nThe app blocks distracting websites and, later, applications. It supports two blocking tiers:\n\n- **Tier 1: Hard Block**\n  - Always blocked.\n  - Cannot be temporarily opened from the normal UI.\n  - Used for sites/apps that should stay unavailable.\n\n- **Tier 2: Controlled Access**\n  - Blocked by default.\n  - Can be opened temporarily under strict rules.\n  - Access can be limited by:\n    - short manual unlocks,\n    - schedules,\n    - daily time allowances,\n    - cooldowns,\n    - maximum session length,\n    - number of unlocks per hour.\n\nThe long-term goal is a blocker that combines browser-level control, a friendly GUI, and system-level enforcement so that blocking cannot be bypassed easily.\nThe name of the App is BlocKuntu","x":860,"y":-180,"width":740,"height":600},
		{"id":"78f716314ea123da","type":"text","text":"## Architecture\nfocus-core/\n  Rust policy library:\n  tiers, schedules, allowances, URL matching, app rules, logs\n\nfocusd/\n  privileged daemon:\n  applies system rules, monitors browser/add-on health, kills blocked apps\n\nfocus-cli/\n  admin/debug CLI:\n  rebuild, status, logs, explain, install-service\n\nfocus-gui/\n  Tauri desktop app:\n  nice UI, calls daemon/core, does not bypass policy\n\nbrowser-extension-firefox/\n  WebExtension:\n  tracks URLs, blocks pages, reports heartbeat/time usage\n\nnative-host/\n  Native Messaging executable:\n  bridge between Firefox extension and Rust backend","x":1760,"y":-942,"width":520,"height":582},
		{"id":"dec34bf03f5eb64b","type":"text","text":"## Blocking Strategy\n\nThe app should use multiple layers:\n\n1. **Browser extension**\n   - Best for URL-level blocking, tracking visits, time limits, and browser-specific rules. It is the main blocker. \n2. **Hosts layer**\n   - Best as a fallback against simple bypasses and non-browser access. Perfect for Tier 1 Blocking. Use chattr +i and watchdog should also check this.\n3. **Systemd watchdog**\n   - Best for restoring enforcement if files or services are modified. See if Browser extension is active. Also kills unwanted programms by reading the window title.\n1. **GUI/backend policy layer**\n   - Best for configuration, schedules, logs, and user-facing control.","x":20,"y":161,"width":693,"height":427},
		{"id":"cc6c23203ae60dc2","type":"text","text":"## MVP Direction\n\nThe next version should focus on these pieces:\n- Tauri GUI as the main user interface.\n- Firefox add-on for browser-level blocking and tracking.\n- Native Messaging bridge between Firefox and the Rust backend.\n- Shared Rust policy engine used by CLI, GUI, add-on bridge, and systemd.\n- Systemd watchdog that verifies:\n  - hosts file correctness,\n  - backend service health,\n  - Firefox add-on heartbeat.\nChrome support and application blocking can come later.","x":920,"y":680,"width":568,"height":400},
		{"id":"e0aa0bea453519a3","type":"file","file":"99 Bilder/Pasted image 20260520231544.png","x":2710,"y":-1395,"width":400,"height":261},
		{"id":"ad7b0f9f99a8935b","type":"file","file":"99 Bilder/Pasted image 20260520231629.png","x":2710,"y":-1075,"width":400,"height":261},
