		{"id":"dff8079dfca1f49b","type":"file","file":"99 Bilder/Pasted image 20260520231753.png","x":3186,"y":-746,"width":477,"height":311},
		{"id":"22b084f484204dd6","type":"file","file":"99 Bilder/Pasted image 20260520231717.png","x":3172,"y":-1075,"width":491,"height":320},
		{"id":"7475135e2e438b74","type":"file","file":"99 Bilder/Pasted image 20260520231705.png","x":3172,"y":-1380,"width":491,"height":283},
		{"id":"7a120bd1253055fe","type":"text","text":"### Desktop GUI\n\nTech: Tauri v2 + Svelte + TypeScript.\n\nWhat it should have:\n- Dashboard: active tier state, running unlocks, extension health, systemd health.\n- Rules editor: Tier 1/Tier 2 sites, URL patterns, apps, required apps.\n- Schedule editor: weekly grid, strict windows, maintenance windows.\n- Allowance editor: daily minutes, max session length, cooldowns, opens/hour.\n- Logs/statistics: visits, blocked attempts, unlocks, app kills, tamper events.\n- Settings: browser integration, service install status, backup/export/import.\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `get_dashboard()`\n- `list_rules()` / `save_rule(rule)` / `delete_rule(id)`\n- `list_schedules()` / `save_schedule(schedule)`\n- `request_unlock(target, minutes, reason)`\n- `close_unlock(unlock_id)`\n- `get_logs(filter)` / `get_stats(range)`\n- `install_services()` / `check_services()`\n\nImportant rule:\nThe GUI never decides allow/block by itself. It only calls the backend API.","x":2190,"y":-220,"width":650,"height":640},
		{"id":"d0b5239b9c52889d","type":"text","text":"### Firefox Extension\n\nTech: WebExtension + TypeScript.\nMain website blocker for Firefox.\n\nWhat it should have:\n- URL blocking before/while pages load.\n- Full URL rules, not only domains.\n- Tier 2 time tracking per tab and per site.\n- Block page with reason, remaining allowance, and possible unlock action.\n- Heartbeat so backend/systemd can verify it is active.\n- Native Messaging connection to local backend.\n- Later: Chrome/Chromium extension with shared rule logic.\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `on_before_navigate(url, tab_id)`\n- `on_tab_activated(tab_id)`\n- `on_url_changed(tab_id, url)`\n- `start_visit(tab_id, url)`\n- `stop_visit(tab_id)`\n- `evaluate_url(url)` via native message\n- `show_block_page(reason)`\n- `request_unlock(url, minutes, reason)`\n- `send_heartbeat()` every few seconds\n\nNative messages:\n- `EvaluateUrl`\n- `VisitStarted`\n- `VisitHeartbeat`\n- `VisitEnded`\n- `UnlockRequested`\n- `ExtensionHeartbeat`","x":2950,"y":-214,"width":650,"height":660},
		{"id":"519359e31cc7156d","type":"text","text":"## Systemd / System Enforcement\n\nTech: systemd units + privileged Rust daemon.\nKeeps the blocker alive and handles system-level actions.\n\nWhat it should have:\n- Start backend daemon on boot.\n- Restart daemon if it crashes.\n- Run schedule ticks regularly.\n- Watch `/etc/hosts` and restore Tier 1 fallback entries.\n- Check Firefox extension heartbeat.\n- Check native messaging host health.\n- Block forbidden programs.\n- Enforce required programs/services.\n- React to tampering in strict mode.\n\nProgram enforcement:\n- Block by executable path, process name, desktop file id, and environment tracking (Fallback to `/proc/[pid]/exe` to bypass Wayland window-title tracking restrictions).\n- Required programs can include `focusd`, native host, Firefox profile/extension, DNS helper.\n- If a blocked program starts: close/kill it and log event.\n- If a required program disappears: restart it or trigger strict action.\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `install_units()` / `uninstall_units()`\n- `watchdog_tick()`\n- `schedule_tick()`\n- `verify_hosts()` / `repair_hosts()`\n- `verify_extension_heartbeat()`\n- `scan_processes()`\n- `kill_blocked_process(pid, reason)`\n- `verify_required_processes()`\n- `handle_tamper_event(event)`\n\nPossible units:\n- `blockuntu.service`\n- `blockuntu-watchdog.service` (Cross-linked partner unit)\n- `blockuntu-watchdog.timer`\n- `blockuntu-hosts.path` (Instant monitoring via Inotify attribute changes)\n- `blockuntu-schedule.timer`","x":2710,"y":510,"width":690,"height":720},
		{"id":"3d3a4129dc1d76f6","type":"text","text":"## Backend / Policy Engine\n\nTech: Rust core library + Rust daemon (`blockuntud`).\nSingle source of truth for all decisions.\n\nWhat it should have:\n- Rule evaluation for domains, URLs, apps, and schedules.\n- Tier 1 hard block logic.\n- Tier 2 unlock, allowance, cooldown, and visit-time logic.\n- Shared API for GUI, CLI, extension bridge, and systemd.\n- SQLite access for runtime state and logs.\n- Config import/export and migration.\n- Hosts fallback rendering for Tier 1.\n- Strict-mode tamper policy.\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `load_config()` / `validate_config()`\n- `migrate_database()`\n- `evaluate_url(url, context) -> Decision`\n- `evaluate_app(process) -> Decision`\n- `request_unlock(target, minutes, reason) -> Unlock`\n- `close_unlock(unlock_id)`\n- `record_visit_start(url, tab_id)`\n- `record_visit_heartbeat(visit_id)`\n- `record_visit_end(visit_id)`\n- `apply_schedule(now)`\n- `render_hosts_block()`\n- `record_event(event)`\n- `get_dashboard_state()`\n- `get_stats(range)`\n\nAPI shape:\n- Local Unix socket JSON-RPC with structural user-group permissions (`0660` via systemd socket activation).\n- Native Messaging JSON protocol for Firefox.\n- No frontend writes config/state directly.","x":3630,"y":-213,"width":720,"height":760},
		{"id":"cfec6212d7ebfe66","type":"text","text":"### Native Messaging Bridge\n\nTech: small Rust executable registered as Firefox native host.\n\nPurpose:\nConnect the Firefox extension to `blockuntud` without giving the extension direct file or privileged access.\n\nWhat it should have:\n- Read JSON messages from Firefox stdin.\n- Validate message shape and extension id.\n- Forward requests to backend Unix socket.\n- Return compact JSON responses.\n- Keep no policy of its own.\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `read_native_message()`\n- `write_native_message(response)`\n- `validate_sender(extension_id)`\n- `forward_to_backend(request)`\n- `map_backend_error(error)`\n\nMessages:\n- `EvaluateUrl`\n- `VisitStarted`\n- `VisitHeartbeat`\n- `VisitEnded`\n- `UnlockRequested`\n- `ExtensionHeartbeat`","x":4370,"y":-211,"width":600,"height":520},
		{"id":"efed881955756d45","type":"text","text":"## Techstack\nCore/backend: Rust\nDaemon: Rust + systemd\nGUI: Tauri v2 + Svelte + TypeScript\nBrowser: Firefox WebExtension (Enforced via `/etc/firefox/policies/policies.json`) + TypeScript\nBridge: Native Messaging host in Rust\nState/logs: SQLite\nConfig: TOML or YAML, with GUI-managed editing\nHard website fallback: `/etc/hosts` for Tier 1\nFuture stronger network layer: local DNS resolver or nftables\nPackaging: `.deb` / AppImage first","x":3411,"y":720,"width":543,"height":307},
		{"id":"9bb777c4dcab74a1","type":"text","text":"### SQLite + Config\n\nUse both, but with different jobs.\n\nConfig file:\n- Human-readable source for durable settings.\n- Good for backups, git, export/import, recovery.\n- Contains sites, app rules, schedules, defaults, strict-mode settings.\n- Prefer TOML or YAML.\n\nSQLite database:\n- Runtime state and history.\n- Good for querying logs/statistics and avoiding fragile JSONL parsing.\n- Contains unlocks, visits, events, allowance usage, extension heartbeat, service health.\n\nSuggested tables:\n- `rules`\n- `rule_patterns`\n- `apps`\n- `schedules`\n- `allowances`\n- `unlocks`\n- `visits`\n- `events`\n- `heartbeats`\n- `service_state`\n\nImplementation functions:\n- `load_config_file()`\n- `save_config_file()`\n- `import_config()` / `export_config()`\n- `migrate_db()`\n- `insert_event()`\n- `upsert_heartbeat(component)`\n- `query_usage(target, range)`\n- `query_active_unlocks()`","x":4370,"y":440,"width":620,"height":680},
		{"id":"6dd2e5d807b33e2c","type":"text","text":"## Component Connections\n\n```mermaid\nflowchart LR\n  User[\"User\"]\n\n  subgraph UserApps[\"Unprivileged user apps\"]\n    GUI[\"Tauri GUI\"]\n    CLI[\"CLI / admin tools\"]\n    Firefox[\"Firefox\"]\n    Extension[\"Firefox WebExtension\"]\n    NativeHost[\"Native Messaging host\"]\n  end\n\n  subgraph BackendLayer[\"Backend layer\"]\n    Daemon[\"blockuntud daemon\"]\n    Core[\"Rust policy core\"]\n    API[\"Local API over Unix socket\"]\n  end\n\n  subgraph DataLayer[\"State and configuration\"]\n    Config[\"Config file TOML or YAML\"]\n    DB[(SQLite database)]\n  end\n\n  subgraph SystemLayer[\"Privileged system enforcement\"]\n    Systemd[\"systemd units\"]\n    Hosts[\"/etc/hosts Tier 1 fallback\"]\n    Processes[\"Linux processes and apps\"]\n    Network[\"Future DNS or nftables layer\"]\n  end\n\n  User -->|edits rules and schedules| GUI\n  User -->|debug/admin commands| CLI\n  User -->|browses sites| Firefox\n\n  Firefox -->|tab and URL events| Extension\n  Extension -->|Native Messaging JSON| NativeHost\n  NativeHost -->|JSON-RPC request| API\n\n  GUI -->|JSON-RPC request| API\n  CLI -->|JSON-RPC request| API\n  API --> Daemon\n  Daemon --> Core\n  Core -->|allow/block/unlock decisions| Daemon\n\n  Core <-->|validated durable settings| Config\n  Core <-->|visits, unlocks, logs, heartbeats| DB\n\n  Systemd -->|start, restart, watchdog, timers| Daemon\n  Daemon -->|health and tamper status| Systemd\n  Systemd -->|path/timer trigger| Daemon\n\n  Daemon -->|render and repair hard blocks| Hosts\n  Daemon -->|scan, kill, restart, verify required apps| Processes\n  Daemon -.->|later stronger network blocking| Network\n\n  Daemon -->|decision response| NativeHost\n  NativeHost -->|allow/block reason and timers| Extension\n  Extension -->|allow page or show block page| Firefox\n```\n\nMain data flow:\n1. User edits rules in the GUI.\n2. GUI sends the change to the backend over a local Unix socket.\n3. Backend validates the change, updates config/SQLite, and recalculates active policy.\n4. Firefox extension sends URL and visit events through Native Messaging.\n5. Backend decides allow/block using tiers, schedules, allowances, cooldowns, and active unlocks.\n6. systemd keeps the daemon alive, runs schedule/watchdog ticks, repairs Tier 1 hosts fallback, and checks tampering.\n7. Backend logs visits, blocks, unlocks, app kills, service health, and tamper events to SQLite.\n\nTrust boundary:\n- GUI, CLI, Firefox, extension, and native host are unprivileged entry points.\n- Backend owns all policy decisions.\n- systemd owns survival, restart, timer, and tamper-response behavior.\n- `/etc/hosts` is only fallback/hard-block support, mainly for Tier 1.\n- SQLite stores runtime truth; config stores durable user intent.","x":5140,"y":-220,"width":3100,"height":1940},
		{"id":"95059b3994cdb901","x":3760,"y":1230,"width":747,"height":594,"type":"text","text":"### Hardened Security & Advanced Tamper Proofing\n\n**1. Cross-Linked Systemd Services**\n- `blockuntu.service` and `blockuntu-watchdog.service` share mutual `BindsTo=` and `Restart=always` properties. \n- Stopping either forcefully instantly triggers its counter-unit via control signals to pull it back up. Use `RestartSec=0` for an instant loop.\n\n**2. Firefox Policy Enforcement**\n- Write configuration parameters directly to `/etc/firefox/policies/policies.json`.\n- Use the `\"ExtensionSettings\"` block with `\"installation_mode\": \"force_installed\"` pointing to your local packed extension or XPI path.\n- This entirely prevents manual deletion or disabling within `about:addons`, and automatically forces the extension to run inside Private Browsing windows.\n\n**3. Unix Domain Socket Privileges**\n- Let systemd handle socket allocation using a separate systemd unit file `blockuntu.socket` configured with file paths under `/run/blockuntu/blockuntud.sock`.\n- Enforce strict access isolation: `SocketMode=0660`, `SocketUser=root`, `SocketGroup=blockuntu`. \n- Unprivileged user desktop applications (like the Native Host or GUI) can then be safely added to the `blockuntu` user group to communicate without compromising root-level security."}
	],
